values
  ('MEDICAL_KNOWLEDGE', (select id from public.knowledge_documents where title = 'Metformin'), 0, 'Metformin
Metformin is a medicine used in the management of type 2 diabetes. It lowers blood glucose mainly by reducing the amount of glucose the liver releases and by making the body''s tissues more responsive to insulin. It does not work by causing the pancreas to release more insulin, which is why on its own it carries a low risk of hypoglycaemia.
Major guidelines commonly describe metformin as a first-line medicine for type 2 diabetes alongside diet and physical activity. Common side effects are digestive and often settle with time or with a slower increase in dose. Kidney function is usually checked before starting and periodically afterwards.
