  ('MEDICAL_KNOWLEDGE', (select id from public.knowledge_documents where title = 'High blood pressure (hypertension)'), 0, 'High blood pressure (hypertension)
Hypertension is persistently raised blood pressure. It usually causes no symptoms, which is why it is often found during routine measurement rather than because someone feels unwell. Over years, sustained high blood pressure increases the risk of stroke, heart disease and kidney disease.
It is identified from repeated readings rather than a single measurement, and guidelines commonly describe confirming clinic readings with measurements taken at home or over 24 hours, because blood pressure is often higher in a clinical setting.
